1
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:05,341
Welcome.

2
00:00:05,341 --> 00:00:12,467
In this course you take seven applications, written in seven different technology stacks:

3
00:00:12,467 --> 00:00:17,591
React, Node.js, Python, Go, Java, Laravel, and plain JavaScript.

4
00:00:17,591 --> 00:00:20,634
Together they form one small platform:

5
00:00:20,634 --> 00:00:21,514
a bookshop.

6
00:00:22,067 --> 00:00:24,758
You package every one of them as a Docker image,

7
00:00:24,758 --> 00:00:29,411
run each image as a container, and then run the whole platform with Docker Compose.

8
00:00:29,967 --> 00:00:33,468
Then you deploy the exact same images to Kubernetes,

9
00:00:33,468 --> 00:00:37,912
and for each application you choose the right Kubernetes resource:

10
00:00:37,912 --> 00:00:43,366
a Deployment, a Service, a ConfigMap, a Secret, a Job, a StatefulSet, an Ingress.

11
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:46,908
And then you do what engineers do every day:

12
00:00:46,908 --> 00:00:51,625
scale, update, roll back, and troubleshoot twelve realistic failures.

13
00:00:53,367 --> 00:00:56,418
Everything comes from one free repository on GitHub.

14
00:00:56,418 --> 00:01:02,167
Each lesson is a Markdown file whose commands are executed automatically on fresh machines in C I.

15
00:01:02,167 --> 00:01:06,333
Every terminal you see in this video is a real recording of those runs.

16
00:01:06,867 --> 00:01:09,437
The course follows a roadmap of twenty levels,

17
00:01:09,437 --> 00:01:14,968
and after each section there are challenges, then twelve troubleshooting labs and a final capstone.

18
00:01:16,733 --> 00:01:18,132
Here is the platform.

19
00:01:18,132 --> 00:01:23,595
Users open the bookshop in the browser, and every request enters through one door:

20
00:01:23,595 --> 00:01:24,395
the Ingress.

21
00:01:24,933 --> 00:01:27,119
The Ingress looks at the path.

22
00:01:27,119 --> 00:01:29,523
Slash goes to the React frontend.

23
00:01:29,523 --> 00:01:33,166
Slash A P I slash users goes to the Node.js A P I,

24
00:01:33,166 --> 00:01:39,140
books to the Java A P I, stats to the Python A P I, status to the Go status board.

25
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:44,183
And requests for the admin host name go to the Laravel reviews admin.

26
00:01:44,183 --> 00:01:46,522
Seven applications, one entry point.

27
00:01:47,067 --> 00:01:52,713
Four of them store their data in one PostgreSQL database, each in its own table:

28
00:01:52,713 --> 00:01:56,806
users from Node.js, books from Java, reviews from Laravel.

29
00:01:57,367 --> 00:02:00,044
And a small JavaScript job runs on a schedule,

30
00:02:00,044 --> 00:02:05,281
collects statistics and the status of all services, and writes a report into the database.

31
00:02:07,033 --> 00:02:11,463
Seven languages could mean seven different ways of doing everything.

32
00:02:11,463 --> 00:02:14,198
So every application follows one contract.

33
00:02:14,198 --> 00:02:14,589
First:

34
00:02:14,589 --> 00:02:17,455
the port comes from an environment variable.

35
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:18,507
Second:

36
00:02:18,507 --> 00:02:22,127
slash health answers as soon as the process works.

37
00:02:22,127 --> 00:02:25,240
It does not check the database, on purpose.

38
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:26,169
Third:

39
00:02:26,169 --> 00:02:29,547
slash ready answers only when the database answers too.

40
00:02:29,547 --> 00:02:33,785
Kubernetes will use these two endpoints in completely different ways.

41
00:02:34,333 --> 00:02:35,035
And fourth:

42
00:02:35,035 --> 00:02:40,393
all configuration comes from environment variables, and all logs go to standard out.

43
00:02:40,393 --> 00:02:43,646
These few rules are what Kubernetes actually needs.

44
00:02:43,646 --> 00:02:46,262
The language behind them does not matter.

45
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:49,160
Why seven stacks?

46
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:52,368
Because that is what a real company looks like.

47
00:02:52,368 --> 00:02:56,532
And because it proves the most important idea of this course.

48
00:02:56,532 --> 00:03:03,017
Kubernetes sees an image, a port, environment variables, health endpoints, logs and exit codes.

49
00:03:03,567 --> 00:03:07,584
It does not see the language, the framework or the build tool.

50
00:03:07,584 --> 00:03:10,629
A Go binary and a Java JAR look the same to it:

51
00:03:10,629 --> 00:03:11,406
a container.

52
00:03:11,967 --> 00:03:15,005
Companies end up with many stacks for good reasons:

53
00:03:15,005 --> 00:03:18,936
each team picks the right tool, old systems live next to new ones.

54
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:23,059
So the skill is not deploying Node.js or deploying Java.

55
00:03:23,059 --> 00:03:28,525
It is a pattern, and once you have used it seven times, you can use it for the eighth.

56
00:03:30,267 --> 00:03:35,489
Three of our seven applications are JavaScript, and that is a lesson on its own.

57
00:03:35,489 --> 00:03:37,121
JavaScript is a language.

58
00:03:37,121 --> 00:03:42,148
In React, it runs in the user's browser; our container only serves the files.

59
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:47,124
Node.js is a runtime that runs JavaScript outside the browser.

60
00:03:47,124 --> 00:03:48,622
node-api is a server:

61
00:03:48,622 --> 00:03:50,620
it runs until it is stopped.

62
00:03:51,167 --> 00:03:55,840
report-worker is JavaScript on Node.js too, but it is a batch job:

63
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:57,751
it does one task and exits.

64
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:01,557
For Kubernetes these are three different workloads:

65
00:04:01,557 --> 00:04:06,218
a Deployment serving files, a Deployment serving an A P I, and a CronJob.

66
00:04:06,218 --> 00:04:09,475
The language is not what decides; the behaviour is.

67
00:04:11,233 --> 00:04:12,804
The first application:

68
00:04:12,804 --> 00:04:15,517
a users A P I in Node.js with Express.

69
00:04:15,517 --> 00:04:20,230
Before anything else, it checks that the database password is set.

70
00:04:20,230 --> 00:04:24,228
If not, it prints a clear message and exits with code 1.

71
00:04:24,767 --> 00:04:27,950
All connection settings come from environment variables.

72
00:04:27,950 --> 00:04:30,962
Nothing about the environment is written in the code.

73
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:34,615
Besides the health endpoints it has two routes:

74
00:04:34,615 --> 00:04:36,405
list users, and add a user.

75
00:04:36,405 --> 00:04:40,382
When it starts, it creates its table and three sample users.

76
00:04:40,382 --> 00:04:43,166
That is all the application logic we need:

77
00:04:43,166 --> 00:04:44,890
the subject is deployment.

78
00:04:46,633 --> 00:04:47,683
The Dockerfile.

79
00:04:47,683 --> 00:04:51,673
Node.js needs no compile step, so this is a single stage:

80
00:04:51,673 --> 00:04:55,733
start from the official Node image, with a pinned version.

81
00:04:56,267 --> 00:05:00,851
Copy only the package files first, and install production dependencies.

82
00:05:00,851 --> 00:05:02,465
Docker caches this layer:

83
00:05:02,465 --> 00:05:05,823
changing your code does not reinstall every package.

84
00:05:06,367 --> 00:05:10,678
Then the source code, the version as a build argument, and the port.

85
00:05:11,233 --> 00:05:11,979
And finally:

86
00:05:11,979 --> 00:05:16,015
run as the unprivileged user node, with the command in exec form,

87
00:05:16,015 --> 00:05:19,803
so Node is process one and receives the stop signal directly.

88
00:05:21,567 --> 00:05:23,979
Build the image and look at it:

89
00:05:23,979 --> 00:05:28,026
about 250 megabytes, most of it the Node.js runtime.

90
00:05:28,567 --> 00:05:34,261
Run it next to a PostgreSQL container, and the A P I answers with the three seeded users.

91
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:40,531
And without a password, it refuses to start, with a message that says exactly what is missing.

92
00:05:40,531 --> 00:05:41,994
Remember this behaviour:

93
00:05:41,994 --> 00:05:45,835
in Kubernetes it turns into a crash loop with an obvious cause.

94
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:50,280
React is the one stack that is not a server.

95
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:55,883
While you develop, Vite's dev server compiles on the fly and reloads the page when you edit.

96
00:05:55,883 --> 00:05:58,563
Great for development, never for production.

97
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:04,468
For production, npm run build turns the whole application into a few static files:

98
00:06:04,468 --> 00:06:06,956
about two hundred and forty kilobytes.

99
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:10,008
Any web server can serve those files.

100
00:06:10,008 --> 00:06:13,396
We use nginx, which also answers the health check,

101
00:06:13,396 --> 00:06:18,411
serves the runtime configuration, and in Compose forwards the A P I calls.

102
00:06:18,967 --> 00:06:20,433
The important insight:

103
00:06:20,433 --> 00:06:23,298
the React code never runs in the container.

104
00:06:23,298 --> 00:06:24,831
It runs in the browser.

105
00:06:24,831 --> 00:06:27,230
The container is just a file server.

106
00:06:28,967 --> 00:06:30,575
The page has four panels:

107
00:06:30,575 --> 00:06:37,074
books from Java, users from Node.js, statistics from Python, and the status of every service from Go.

108
00:06:37,074 --> 00:06:42,993
Each panel shows its own error when its backend is missing, which will help us troubleshoot.

109
00:06:44,733 --> 00:06:46,785
This Dockerfile has two stages.

110
00:06:46,785 --> 00:06:51,880
The first one starts from Node, installs the dependencies and runs the build.

111
00:06:52,433 --> 00:06:58,275
The second stage starts fresh from nginx, and copies only the built files from the first.

112
00:06:58,275 --> 00:07:02,345
Node.js, the dependencies and the source code are left behind.

113
00:07:02,900 --> 00:07:03,777
One more trick:

114
00:07:03,777 --> 00:07:09,505
the settings, like the admin link, are written into a small config file when the container starts.

115
00:07:09,505 --> 00:07:14,941
A normal React build bakes them in at build time, which would need one image per environment.

116
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:17,370
The result:

117
00:07:17,370 --> 00:07:22,667
the build stage is three hundred and thirty-four megabytes, the final image eighty-two.

118
00:07:22,667 --> 00:07:25,284
What you ship is only what you need to run.

119
00:07:25,833 --> 00:07:29,933
The runtime configuration, generated when the container started.

120
00:07:30,467 --> 00:07:36,273
And with no backend running, an A P I call returns a clean five oh two with a JSON message,

121
00:07:36,273 --> 00:07:38,506
instead of nginx refusing to start.

122
00:07:40,267 --> 00:07:42,570
The Python A P I uses FastAPI.

123
00:07:42,570 --> 00:07:44,566
Slash health just answers.

124
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:50,839
Slash ready runs a tiny query against the database and returns five oh three with the reason if it fails.

125
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:56,990
And slash A P I slash stats counts the users, books and reviews that the other services store.

126
00:07:56,990 --> 00:08:02,996
A table that does not exist yet counts as zero, so the order in which services start does not matter.

127
00:08:04,733 --> 00:08:10,205
Python is interpreted, but its dependencies can include compiled parts and build tools.

128
00:08:10,205 --> 00:08:14,105
So we install them in a virtual environment, in a build stage.

129
00:08:14,667 --> 00:08:19,200
The final stage starts clean and copies only the virtual environment and the code.

130
00:08:19,733 --> 00:08:21,085
Same rules as before:

131
00:08:21,085 --> 00:08:27,460
a fixed user I D instead of root, the version as a build argument, logs unbuffered to standard out.

132
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:33,924
Running against PostgreSQL, the statistics endpoint counts the rows.

133
00:08:34,467 --> 00:08:35,977
Now stop the database.

134
00:08:35,977 --> 00:08:38,312
Slash ready answers five oh three:

135
00:08:38,312 --> 00:08:39,617
I cannot do my job.

136
00:08:39,617 --> 00:08:42,295
Slash health still answers two hundred:

137
00:08:42,295 --> 00:08:43,050
I am alive.

138
00:08:43,050 --> 00:08:49,436
In Kubernetes, that difference decides between taking a Pod out of traffic and restarting it.

139
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:53,287
The Go service is the status board.

140
00:08:53,287 --> 00:08:57,402
It reads the list of services to check from one environment variable.

141
00:08:57,967 --> 00:09:02,598
For each target it starts a goroutine, so all the health checks run in parallel,

142
00:09:02,598 --> 00:09:06,418
each with a two second timeout, and then it waits for all of them.

143
00:09:06,967 --> 00:09:10,473
And it uses nothing but Go's standard library.

144
00:09:10,473 --> 00:09:12,760
No framework, no dependencies.

145
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:16,469
Go compiles to a single binary.

146
00:09:16,469 --> 00:09:23,266
The build stage uses the full Go toolchain and builds a static, stripped binary, with the version baked in.

147
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:25,828
The final stage is distroless:

148
00:09:25,828 --> 00:09:30,764
no shell, no package manager, nothing but our binary and a non-root user.

149
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:33,764
The final image:

150
00:09:33,764 --> 00:09:35,659
about sixteen megabytes.

151
00:09:36,200 --> 00:09:38,672
The toolchain image it was built with:

152
00:09:38,672 --> 00:09:40,298
three hundred and eighty.

153
00:09:40,298 --> 00:09:41,859
None of that is shipped.

154
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:44,365
And there is no shell inside.

155
00:09:44,365 --> 00:09:46,060
Trying to open one fails.

156
00:09:46,060 --> 00:09:47,890
That is a security feature:

157
00:09:47,890 --> 00:09:51,346
an attacker who gets in finds nothing to work with.

158
00:09:53,100 --> 00:09:56,116
The books A P I in Java, with Spring Boot.

159
00:09:56,116 --> 00:09:58,845
A plain controller with plain J D B C:

160
00:09:58,845 --> 00:10:01,574
as small as a Spring application gets.

161
00:10:02,133 --> 00:10:03,822
The same contract again:

162
00:10:03,822 --> 00:10:07,198
slash health, and slash ready with a real query.

163
00:10:07,733 --> 00:10:09,076
And one design choice:

164
00:10:09,076 --> 00:10:13,959
it starts even when the database is unreachable, and reports why in slash ready.

165
00:10:13,959 --> 00:10:19,513
A service that refuses to start because a dependency is briefly down causes restart storms.

166
00:10:21,267 --> 00:10:22,894
Java has a real build:

167
00:10:22,894 --> 00:10:28,072
Maven downloads the dependencies, compiles, and packages one JAR file.

168
00:10:28,072 --> 00:10:30,956
Dependencies first, so they are cached.

169
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:36,350
The final stage needs only a Java runtime, not the compiler and not Maven.

170
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:39,188
And one line matters a lot in containers:

171
00:10:39,188 --> 00:10:43,875
max RAM percentage tells the JVM to size its heap from the container's memory limit,

172
00:10:43,875 --> 00:10:46,163
not from the memory of the whole machine.

173
00:10:47,900 --> 00:10:52,267
The build stage with Maven and the full JDK, against the runtime image:

174
00:10:52,267 --> 00:10:54,419
the runtime is about half the size.

175
00:10:54,967 --> 00:10:56,052
And the startup:

176
00:10:56,052 --> 00:10:59,986
a few seconds on a free CPU, more when the CPU is limited.

177
00:10:59,986 --> 00:11:05,277
Kubernetes must know that, or it will kill the JVM while it is still starting.

178
00:11:05,277 --> 00:11:07,448
We will give it a startup probe.

179
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:15,605
Laravel is the most complex stack here, because PHP usually runs behind a web server.

180
00:11:15,605 --> 00:11:24,044
nginx handles H T T P and static files, and passes PHP requests to PHP F P M over FastCGI on port nine thousand.

181
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:29,865
PHP F P M runs the Laravel code, which reads and writes the reviews table.

182
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:36,295
And database migrations are a separate step that runs once, with the same image, and exits.

183
00:11:36,295 --> 00:11:39,534
Never in every copy of the application at startup.

184
00:11:40,100 --> 00:11:43,884
Two containers, because each container should run one process.

185
00:11:43,884 --> 00:11:47,485
In Kubernetes they will become one Pod with two containers.

186
00:11:49,233 --> 00:11:50,184
The PHP image:

187
00:11:50,184 --> 00:11:55,752
Composer installs the dependencies in a first stage, without development packages.

188
00:11:56,300 --> 00:12:01,686
The final stage is the official PHP F P M image with the PostgreSQL extension,

189
00:12:01,686 --> 00:12:05,276
the application, and the unprivileged www-data user.

190
00:12:05,833 --> 00:12:11,089
Laravel can cache its configuration for speed, but we deliberately do not do that at build time:

191
00:12:11,089 --> 00:12:15,415
the values come from the environment and differ between Compose and Kubernetes.

192
00:12:17,167 --> 00:12:18,241
Two images:

193
00:12:18,241 --> 00:12:22,441
PHP F P M, and nginx with the public files.

194
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:29,170
The migration runs once in a throw-away container, creates the reviews table and adds three sample reviews.

195
00:12:29,733 --> 00:12:31,341
And the classic failure:

196
00:12:31,341 --> 00:12:35,965
nginx without a reachable PHP F P M answers five oh two, bad gateway.

197
00:12:35,965 --> 00:12:39,583
Its log says connection refused to port nine thousand.

198
00:12:39,583 --> 00:12:42,465
Now you know where to look when you see it.

199
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:49,732
Seven containers that must find each other, share a database and start in a sensible order.

200
00:12:49,732 --> 00:12:52,710
Docker Compose describes all of that in one file.

201
00:12:52,710 --> 00:12:53,926
Here is one service:

202
00:12:53,926 --> 00:12:57,270
where to build it, and the image name with its version.

203
00:12:57,833 --> 00:12:58,883
The environment:

204
00:12:58,883 --> 00:13:02,753
the same database settings for every service, defined once.

205
00:13:02,753 --> 00:13:06,952
The password comes from a dot env file that never goes into Git.

206
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:08,424
Then the order:

207
00:13:08,424 --> 00:13:13,721
start after PostgreSQL is healthy, and count as healthy only when slash ready answers.

208
00:13:13,721 --> 00:13:20,496
Without health conditions, depends on only waits until the other container has started, not until it is ready.

209
00:13:21,033 --> 00:13:25,383
And the networks this service joins, which decide who can reach it by name.

210
00:13:27,133 --> 00:13:30,959
One command builds what is missing and starts everything in order.

211
00:13:30,959 --> 00:13:34,958
With wait, it returns only when every service is running and healthy.

212
00:13:35,500 --> 00:13:38,424
Nine containers from seven stacks, all healthy.

213
00:13:38,424 --> 00:13:42,904
Only two of them publish a port on this computer, and only on localhost:

214
00:13:42,904 --> 00:13:45,454
the user interface and the reviews admin.

215
00:13:45,454 --> 00:13:48,316
Everything else is reachable only from inside.

216
00:13:50,067 --> 00:13:51,454
Through the frontend:

217
00:13:51,454 --> 00:13:54,296
books from Java and statistics from Python.

218
00:13:54,833 --> 00:13:56,453
The Go status board:

219
00:13:56,453 --> 00:13:58,315
six of six services up.

220
00:13:58,867 --> 00:14:03,779
And the JavaScript worker is not a server, so it does not start with the rest.

221
00:14:03,779 --> 00:14:07,936
Docker compose run starts it once; it writes its report and exits.

222
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:13,481
The frontend container is only on the frontend network.

223
00:14:13,481 --> 00:14:16,436
It needs to reach the A P Is, nothing else.

224
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,010
The A P Is are on both networks:

225
00:14:19,010 --> 00:14:22,215
they talk to the frontend side and to the database.

226
00:14:22,767 --> 00:14:27,918
The database, PHP F P M and the workers are only on the backend network.

227
00:14:28,467 --> 00:14:31,784
And within a network, a service name is a host name.

228
00:14:31,784 --> 00:14:36,441
That is service discovery, and Kubernetes will do the same with Services.

229
00:14:38,200 --> 00:14:43,850
From inside node-api, the Java A P I is simply java-api, port eighty eighty.

230
00:14:44,400 --> 00:14:49,283
From inside the frontend, the name postgres does not even exist:

231
00:14:49,283 --> 00:14:50,199
bad address.

232
00:14:50,199 --> 00:14:51,725
The isolation works.

233
00:14:52,267 --> 00:14:57,581
And in the database, the tables created by three different applications in three languages.

234
00:14:59,333 --> 00:15:01,325
This is the heart of the course.

235
00:15:01,325 --> 00:15:04,809
Kubernetes is not docker compose up on a bigger machine.

236
00:15:04,809 --> 00:15:10,658
Every line of the Compose file maps to a Kubernetes resource, chosen by what the service does.

237
00:15:10,658 --> 00:15:16,943
A server becomes a Deployment, the database a StatefulSet, the worker a CronJob, the migration a Job.

238
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:22,495
Ports become Services inside the cluster, and an Ingress for traffic from outside.

239
00:15:23,033 --> 00:15:27,650
The environment splits into a ConfigMap for settings and Secrets for passwords.

240
00:15:28,200 --> 00:15:31,115
Volumes become persistent volume claims.

241
00:15:31,667 --> 00:15:34,362
Health checks and depends on become probes.

242
00:15:34,362 --> 00:15:36,744
There is no start order in Kubernetes:

243
00:15:36,744 --> 00:15:40,818
everything starts, and readiness decides when a Pod gets traffic.

244
00:15:41,367 --> 00:15:47,646
And instead of restart policies, Kubernetes keeps the declared number of replicas running, on any node.

245
00:15:49,400 --> 00:15:51,439
Here is node-api in both worlds.

246
00:15:51,439 --> 00:15:54,753
The same image, the same port, the same environment,

247
00:15:54,753 --> 00:15:59,341
but in Kubernetes split into references to the ConfigMap and the Secret,

248
00:15:59,341 --> 00:16:03,929
plus a liveness and a readiness probe, resources and a security context.

249
00:16:03,929 --> 00:16:08,518
Longer, yes, but every line answers a question Compose never had to ask:

250
00:16:08,518 --> 00:16:12,086
what happens on a cluster of machines, when things fail.

251
00:16:13,833 --> 00:16:15,153
First the ground work.

252
00:16:15,153 --> 00:16:19,773
kind creates a real Kubernetes one point thirty-seven cluster with two nodes,

253
00:16:19,773 --> 00:16:22,233
each a Docker container on this computer.

254
00:16:22,233 --> 00:16:25,053
Traefik is installed as the ingress controller.

255
00:16:25,600 --> 00:16:29,477
A namespace for the platform, a ConfigMap with the settings,

256
00:16:29,477 --> 00:16:33,871
and the Secrets, created from random values directly in the cluster.

257
00:16:33,871 --> 00:16:36,649
The repository contains no password at all:

258
00:16:36,649 --> 00:16:38,523
describe shows only the size.

259
00:16:39,067 --> 00:16:45,096
And PostgreSQL as a StatefulSet, with a persistent volume claim that is bound to a real volume.

260
00:16:46,833 --> 00:16:49,869
Our image exists only in Docker on this computer.

261
00:16:49,869 --> 00:16:52,657
kind load copies it into the cluster's nodes.

262
00:16:52,657 --> 00:16:55,816
In a cloud you would push it to a registry instead.

263
00:16:56,367 --> 00:16:59,011
Apply the Deployment and the Service.

264
00:16:59,011 --> 00:17:02,871
Two Pods, each with its own I P address, both running.

265
00:17:03,433 --> 00:17:06,945
The Service gives them one stable name and virtual I P.

266
00:17:06,945 --> 00:17:09,500
Its endpoints are the two Pod addresses.

267
00:17:10,033 --> 00:17:14,795
And through the Service, by name, the users come back from PostgreSQL.

268
00:17:14,795 --> 00:17:16,700
node-api runs on Kubernetes.

269
00:17:18,433 --> 00:17:19,278
The frontend:

270
00:17:19,278 --> 00:17:22,593
two replicas of the same image that ran in Compose.

271
00:17:23,133 --> 00:17:24,487
Through a port forward:

272
00:17:24,487 --> 00:17:27,726
the page, its health check, and the configuration file,

273
00:17:27,726 --> 00:17:33,672
which now says environment Kubernetes, because it came from the ConfigMap when the container started.

274
00:17:34,233 --> 00:17:37,522
The users come back through the frontend's proxy.

275
00:17:37,522 --> 00:17:38,999
The books do not, yet:

276
00:17:38,999 --> 00:17:43,697
java-api is not deployed, and the frontend says so with a clean error.

277
00:17:45,433 --> 00:17:50,340
The Python Deployment differs from the Node.js one in a handful of lines:

278
00:17:50,340 --> 00:17:54,978
the name, the image, the port, the user I D and the resource numbers.

279
00:17:54,978 --> 00:17:57,062
Same shape, different language.

280
00:17:57,600 --> 00:17:58,969
It counts three users.

281
00:17:58,969 --> 00:18:01,021
Books and reviews are still zero:

282
00:18:01,021 --> 00:18:07,180
their owners are not deployed yet, and the A P I treats a missing table as zero instead of failing.

283
00:18:08,933 --> 00:18:12,101
The status board's targets come from the ConfigMap:

284
00:18:12,101 --> 00:18:16,450
the same service names as in Compose, resolved by the cluster's D N S.

285
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,359
What is deployed is up, what is not shows as down:

286
00:18:20,359 --> 00:18:22,240
its name does not exist yet.

287
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:27,165
And the distroless container has no shell, in Kubernetes as in Docker.

288
00:18:28,900 --> 00:18:32,435
Java needs a few seconds to start, more with a CPU limit.

289
00:18:32,435 --> 00:18:35,350
If the liveness probe started checking at once,

290
00:18:35,350 --> 00:18:40,002
it could kill the JVM before it ever finished starting, in an endless loop.

291
00:18:40,002 --> 00:18:46,080
The startup probe gives it up to sixty seconds, and holds the other probes back until it succeeds.

292
00:18:46,633 --> 00:18:48,763
After that, liveness takes over:

293
00:18:48,763 --> 00:18:52,490
restart only if slash health fails three times in a row.

294
00:18:53,033 --> 00:18:56,988
And readiness decides about traffic, every five seconds.

295
00:18:58,733 --> 00:19:01,383
Spring Boot reports its own start time.

296
00:19:01,933 --> 00:19:05,763
And the Pod's events show the startup probe failing at first,

297
00:19:05,763 --> 00:19:09,090
connection refused, while the JVM was still starting.

298
00:19:09,090 --> 00:19:11,037
That is exactly what it is for:

299
00:19:11,037 --> 00:19:13,548
those failures did not restart anything.

300
00:19:14,100 --> 00:19:18,126
Then the books arrive, from the table Java created at startup.

301
00:19:19,867 --> 00:19:22,583
Laravel brings two new Kubernetes ideas.

302
00:19:22,583 --> 00:19:28,288
Migrations run once per release, not in every replica at startup, so they are a Job:

303
00:19:28,288 --> 00:19:30,461
it runs to completion and stops.

304
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:37,941
And nginx and PHP F P M always run and scale together, and nginx must reach exactly its own F P M.

305
00:19:37,941 --> 00:19:40,633
So they are two containers in one Pod.

306
00:19:40,633 --> 00:19:47,000
Containers in a Pod share a network, so nginx talks to F P M on one two seven dot zero dot zero dot one.

307
00:19:48,533 --> 00:19:50,840
In Compose they were two services.

308
00:19:50,840 --> 00:19:53,418
In Kubernetes they are a sidecar pair.

309
00:19:55,167 --> 00:19:56,687
The migration Job:

310
00:19:56,687 --> 00:19:59,981
complete, one of one, in a few seconds.

311
00:20:00,533 --> 00:20:01,826
The Laravel Pod:

312
00:20:01,826 --> 00:20:04,493
ready two of two, two containers.

313
00:20:05,033 --> 00:20:07,745
And the JavaScript worker as a CronJob.

314
00:20:07,745 --> 00:20:10,318
We create one Job from it right away:

315
00:20:10,318 --> 00:20:15,046
completed, exit code zero, report saved with six of six services up.

316
00:20:15,046 --> 00:20:17,063
Every application is running.

317
00:20:18,800 --> 00:20:20,345
Pods are replaceable:

318
00:20:20,345 --> 00:20:24,317
when one dies, a new one comes with a new I P address.

319
00:20:24,317 --> 00:20:26,744
So nobody talks to Pods directly.

320
00:20:26,744 --> 00:20:29,025
Clients call a Service by name.

321
00:20:29,567 --> 00:20:33,582
The Service has a stable name and virtual I P, and a selector:

322
00:20:33,582 --> 00:20:35,136
which Pods belong to it.

323
00:20:35,700 --> 00:20:39,418
Its endpoints are the Pods that match the selector and are Ready.

324
00:20:39,418 --> 00:20:42,335
Kubernetes keeps that list up to date all the time.

325
00:20:42,900 --> 00:20:44,255
Remember this sentence:

326
00:20:44,255 --> 00:20:47,850
endpoints are the ready Pods whose labels match the selector.

327
00:20:47,850 --> 00:20:50,915
Half of all networking problems are explained by it.

328
00:20:51,467 --> 00:20:57,576
And the names are the same as in Compose, so not a single line of application configuration had to change.

329
00:20:59,333 --> 00:21:01,512
All the Services of the platform.

330
00:21:01,512 --> 00:21:04,154
Each has a cluster I P, except postgres:

331
00:21:04,154 --> 00:21:08,578
a headless Service, whose name points straight at the database Pod.

332
00:21:09,133 --> 00:21:14,302
Ten requests to the users A P I through one Service name land on both node-api Pods.

333
00:21:14,302 --> 00:21:18,425
The Service balances the load; nobody configured anything for that.

334
00:21:20,167 --> 00:21:22,763
Change the admin link in the ConfigMap.

335
00:21:23,300 --> 00:21:25,429
The page still shows the old value.

336
00:21:25,429 --> 00:21:29,381
Environment variables are copied into a container when it starts.

337
00:21:29,381 --> 00:21:33,882
Changing the ConfigMap does not change processes that are already running.

338
00:21:34,433 --> 00:21:40,918
A rollout restart replaces the Pods one by one, with no downtime, and the new Pods read the new value.

339
00:21:42,667 --> 00:21:45,773
With a throw-away example, never a real secret:

340
00:21:45,773 --> 00:21:52,250
a Secret stores its value base64 encoded, and anyone who may read it can decode it in one command.

341
00:21:52,250 --> 00:21:54,761
Base64 is an encoding, not encryption.

342
00:21:54,761 --> 00:21:57,471
What protects a Secret is access control,

343
00:21:57,471 --> 00:22:02,361
keeping it out of images and Git, and encryption at rest in real clusters.

344
00:22:02,900 --> 00:22:06,062
Our manifests only reference the Secret by name.

345
00:22:06,062 --> 00:22:09,091
The password itself never appears in any file.

346
00:22:10,833 --> 00:22:14,488
Now the difference between liveness and readiness, live.

347
00:22:14,488 --> 00:22:16,250
Scale the database to zero.

348
00:22:16,250 --> 00:22:19,709
Within seconds, every A P I that needs it is running,

349
00:22:19,709 --> 00:22:23,559
but zero of one ready, and the restart count stays at zero.

350
00:22:23,559 --> 00:22:26,691
The processes are fine, so nothing is restarted:

351
00:22:26,691 --> 00:22:29,628
restarting would not bring the database back.

352
00:22:30,167 --> 00:22:35,475
But they are taken out of their Services, so the Ingress has nobody to send traffic to:

353
00:22:35,475 --> 00:22:37,611
five oh three, no available server.

354
00:22:38,167 --> 00:22:39,981
Scale the database back up.

355
00:22:39,981 --> 00:22:41,661
Nobody restarts anything:

356
00:22:41,661 --> 00:22:46,836
the readiness probes simply pass again, and the A P Is rejoin their Services.

357
00:22:48,600 --> 00:22:49,869
And the other side:

358
00:22:49,869 --> 00:22:56,146
point go-status's liveness probe at a path that does not exist, the most common typo there is.

359
00:22:56,146 --> 00:22:58,550
Four oh four, liveness probe failed.

360
00:22:59,100 --> 00:23:04,287
After three failures the kubelet kills the container and starts it again, and again.

361
00:23:04,287 --> 00:23:09,041
A perfectly healthy application, restarted forever by one wrong line of YAML.

362
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:11,833
Add a user.

363
00:23:11,833 --> 00:23:14,745
Then delete the PostgreSQL Pod.

364
00:23:15,300 --> 00:23:22,345
The StatefulSet creates it again, with the same name, postgres zero, and binds it to the same volume claim.

365
00:23:22,900 --> 00:23:24,696
And the user is still there.

366
00:23:24,696 --> 00:23:28,672
The data lives in the persistent volume, not in the container.

367
00:23:28,672 --> 00:23:32,327
A lab database like this is not production ready, though:

368
00:23:32,327 --> 00:23:35,470
real ones need backups, replication and upgrades.

369
00:23:37,233 --> 00:23:43,113
The Ingress is a set of routing rules, and the ingress controller, Traefik, carries them out.

370
00:23:43,667 --> 00:23:50,012
For the bookshop host, each A P I path goes to its Service, and everything else to the frontend.

371
00:23:50,012 --> 00:23:52,061
The longest matching path wins.

372
00:23:52,600 --> 00:23:55,838
And a second host name routes to the Laravel admin.

373
00:23:55,838 --> 00:23:58,060
Seven applications behind one port.

374
00:23:59,800 --> 00:24:02,326
The same U R Ls a browser uses:

375
00:24:02,326 --> 00:24:08,030
the page, users from Node.js, books from Java, statistics from Python.

376
00:24:08,567 --> 00:24:13,948
The status board says six of six services up, and the admin host serves the Laravel page.

377
00:24:15,700 --> 00:24:17,123
And in the browser:

378
00:24:17,123 --> 00:24:23,191
books, users, statistics with the latest report, and the status of every service.

379
00:24:23,191 --> 00:24:26,936
Seven stacks, one platform, running on Kubernetes.

380
00:24:28,700 --> 00:24:30,252
Scaling is one number.

381
00:24:30,252 --> 00:24:32,157
Three replicas of node-api:

382
00:24:32,157 --> 00:24:34,979
three identical Pods behind one Service.

383
00:24:34,979 --> 00:24:39,635
More capacity, and one Pod or one node can fail without an outage.

384
00:24:40,200 --> 00:24:46,241
In the labs you scale your own service to four, and the logs show how twenty requests spread across all four.

385
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:52,177
Requests are what the scheduler reserves, limits are the ceiling.

386
00:24:52,177 --> 00:24:55,518
Give python-api a twenty-four megabyte memory limit,

387
00:24:55,518 --> 00:25:00,401
and the new Pod is O O M killed, exit code one three seven, again and again.

388
00:25:00,967 --> 00:25:02,863
But the statistics still work.

389
00:25:02,863 --> 00:25:09,816
The change started a rolling update, the new Pod never became ready, so Kubernetes never removed the old ones.

390
00:25:10,367 --> 00:25:13,388
The rollout simply stopped at the first bad Pod.

391
00:25:13,388 --> 00:25:16,031
That is the safety net of rolling updates.

392
00:25:17,767 --> 00:25:20,295
A new version is a new image with a new tag.

393
00:25:20,295 --> 00:25:22,708
Kubectl set image starts a rolling update:

394
00:25:22,708 --> 00:25:27,361
new Pods come up and must pass their readiness probe before old ones are removed.

395
00:25:27,900 --> 00:25:34,487
Behind the scenes, a new ReplicaSet for the new Pod template, and the old one kept, scaled to zero.

396
00:25:34,487 --> 00:25:37,282
The new Pods answer version one point one.

397
00:25:37,833 --> 00:25:39,370
And the rollout history:

398
00:25:39,370 --> 00:25:42,443
revision two, with the change cause we recorded.

399
00:25:44,200 --> 00:25:46,984
Version one point one turns out to be bad.

400
00:25:46,984 --> 00:25:53,480
Rollout undo scales the previous ReplicaSet back up, with the same rolling, zero downtime process.

401
00:25:54,033 --> 00:25:56,743
Back on node-api one point zero point zero.

402
00:25:56,743 --> 00:26:00,146
This only works because every version has its own tag:

403
00:26:00,146 --> 00:26:04,683
one point zero point zero still means exactly the image that ran before.

404
00:26:04,683 --> 00:26:08,338
With latest, the previous version would not exist anymore.

405
00:26:10,100 --> 00:26:14,576
Twelve labs break the platform on purpose, and one method solves them all:

406
00:26:14,576 --> 00:26:17,539
the Pod's status tells you which question to ask.

407
00:26:17,539 --> 00:26:19,958
Pending means the scheduler or a volume:

408
00:26:19,958 --> 00:26:22,196
describe the Pod and read its events.

409
00:26:22,733 --> 00:26:25,222
Error image pull and image pull back off:

410
00:26:25,222 --> 00:26:28,258
the image name, the tag, or where the image lives.

411
00:26:28,800 --> 00:26:30,237
Crash loop back off:

412
00:26:30,237 --> 00:26:31,889
the application itself.

413
00:26:31,889 --> 00:26:35,410
Read the logs of the previous, crashed container.

414
00:26:35,967 --> 00:26:37,451
Running but not ready:

415
00:26:37,451 --> 00:26:40,351
the readiness probe, or whatever it checks.

416
00:26:40,900 --> 00:26:42,755
And ready but unreachable:

417
00:26:42,755 --> 00:26:48,036
the Service, its selector, its endpoints, its ports, and then the Ingress.

418
00:26:49,800 --> 00:26:50,328
Lab one:

419
00:26:50,328 --> 00:26:51,979
a typo in the image name.

420
00:26:51,979 --> 00:26:56,402
The new Pod cannot pull the image, while the old Pod keeps serving:

421
00:26:56,402 --> 00:26:58,515
the rolling update protects you.

422
00:26:59,067 --> 00:27:00,402
Lab two is subtler:

423
00:27:00,402 --> 00:27:02,862
the image exists, on your computer.

424
00:27:02,862 --> 00:27:06,447
But the cluster's nodes have their own image store.

425
00:27:06,447 --> 00:27:11,859
The node does not have python-api one point two, tries Docker Hub, and fails.

426
00:27:11,859 --> 00:27:14,881
The fix is kind load, or in a real cluster:

427
00:27:14,881 --> 00:27:16,216
push to a registry.

428
00:27:17,967 --> 00:27:18,556
Lab four:

429
00:27:18,556 --> 00:27:23,006
five oh three through the Ingress, and the Service has no endpoints.

430
00:27:23,006 --> 00:27:24,184
All Pods are fine.

431
00:27:24,184 --> 00:27:28,439
The Service's selector has one extra letter, and selects nothing.

432
00:27:28,439 --> 00:27:32,431
Nothing in Kubernetes checks that labels and selectors match.

433
00:27:32,967 --> 00:27:33,650
Lab five:

434
00:27:33,650 --> 00:27:35,169
crash loop back off.

435
00:27:35,169 --> 00:27:40,408
The current container has no useful log, so ask for the previous one:

436
00:27:40,408 --> 00:27:41,851
cannot find module.

437
00:27:41,851 --> 00:27:43,521
A typo in the command.

438
00:27:45,267 --> 00:27:45,886
Lab seven:

439
00:27:45,886 --> 00:27:52,322
one wrong letter in the database host name in the ConfigMap, and after a restart the A P I is not ready:

440
00:27:52,322 --> 00:27:53,931
the name does not resolve.

441
00:27:54,467 --> 00:27:55,157
Lab eleven:

442
00:27:55,157 --> 00:27:57,794
the frontend cannot reach the books A P I,

443
00:27:57,794 --> 00:28:03,633
five oh two from nginx, because the Service's port no longer matches what the frontend calls.

444
00:28:04,167 --> 00:28:04,865
Lab twelve:

445
00:28:04,865 --> 00:28:08,612
someone replaced the database Secret with a wrong password.

446
00:28:08,612 --> 00:28:10,580
Password authentication failed.

447
00:28:10,580 --> 00:28:15,915
The lab restores it from the database's own configuration, without ever printing it.

448
00:28:17,667 --> 00:28:19,816
Twelve labs in four families:

449
00:28:19,816 --> 00:28:23,224
images, the application, networking, and data.

450
00:28:23,224 --> 00:28:25,225
Each one in the same order:

451
00:28:25,225 --> 00:28:31,153
problem, symptoms, investigation, root cause, fix, verification, lesson learned.

452
00:28:31,153 --> 00:28:33,080
Do them in the repository:

453
00:28:33,080 --> 00:28:38,193
the fix is only half the lesson; the investigation is the other half.

454
00:28:39,933 --> 00:28:40,776
The capstone.

455
00:28:40,776 --> 00:28:44,858
One script does everything the lessons did, in the right order:

456
00:28:44,858 --> 00:28:49,004
build the images, create the cluster and the ingress controller,

457
00:28:49,004 --> 00:28:53,216
load the images, create the namespace, configuration and Secrets,

458
00:28:53,216 --> 00:28:55,937
start the database, run the migration Job,

459
00:28:55,937 --> 00:29:00,538
deploy every application, create the Ingress, and run the first report.

460
00:29:00,538 --> 00:29:01,898
And it is idempotent:

461
00:29:01,898 --> 00:29:04,036
running it twice changes nothing.

462
00:29:05,800 --> 00:29:09,674
And a verification script checks every capability of the course:

463
00:29:09,674 --> 00:29:12,216
all workloads available, the volume bound,

464
00:29:12,216 --> 00:29:15,243
the Job complete, resources and probes everywhere,

465
00:29:15,243 --> 00:29:18,633
no latest tags, non-root, the password only in a Secret,

466
00:29:18,633 --> 00:29:25,291
every route through the Ingress, the status board all up, and data that survives the loss of the database Pod.

467
00:29:27,033 --> 00:29:29,111
Cleaning up is part of the work.

468
00:29:29,111 --> 00:29:34,304
One namespace for the whole platform means one command removes everything in it:

469
00:29:34,304 --> 00:29:38,783
Deployments, Services, the StatefulSet, its volume, Jobs and Secrets.

470
00:29:39,333 --> 00:29:43,062
And deleting the kind cluster removes the nodes themselves.

471
00:29:43,062 --> 00:29:47,928
In Compose, down with dash v removes the containers, networks and the volume.

472
00:29:49,667 --> 00:29:50,942
The final challenge.

473
00:29:50,942 --> 00:29:55,980
A script makes three changes to the running platform, like an eventful weekend.

474
00:29:55,980 --> 00:30:02,101
Statistics are missing, and two rollouts are stuck, hidden by old Pods that still serve traffic.

475
00:30:02,667 --> 00:30:08,002
You get the same tools as in the labs, and you are done when the verification passes again.

476
00:30:08,002 --> 00:30:10,113
Run it before you read the solution.

477
00:30:11,867 --> 00:30:13,919
The verification finds the damage:

478
00:30:13,919 --> 00:30:18,626
a Service with no endpoints, and Deployments stuck in the middle of a rollout.

479
00:30:18,626 --> 00:30:24,662
A selector typo, an image tag that was never built, and a misspelled database name in the ConfigMap.

480
00:30:25,200 --> 00:30:26,069
Three fixes:

481
00:30:26,069 --> 00:30:31,208
apply the Service, undo the rollout, restore the ConfigMap and restart.

482
00:30:31,208 --> 00:30:33,670
And the verification passes again.

483
00:30:35,433 --> 00:30:37,242
Look at what you can do now.

484
00:30:37,242 --> 00:30:41,633
Package any application as a container image, whatever its language.

485
00:30:42,167 --> 00:30:44,971
Run a whole platform with Docker Compose.

486
00:30:45,533 --> 00:30:50,008
Translate it into Kubernetes, choosing the right resource for each job.

487
00:30:50,567 --> 00:30:51,501
Operate it:

488
00:30:51,501 --> 00:30:56,426
probes, resources, scaling, rolling updates and rollbacks.

489
00:30:56,967 --> 00:31:00,971
And troubleshoot it, from the Pod status to the root cause.

490
00:31:00,971 --> 00:31:03,008
Kubernetes manages containers.

491
00:31:03,008 --> 00:31:05,587
Now you know how to give it good ones.

492
00:31:05,587 --> 00:31:08,370
Thanks for watching, and happy deploying.
